'<!-- Reads two CSV files, one as a high performance matrix, to show what a "#read" keeps when the worksheet is compiled.
'Compiling to a "*.cpdz" gives each directive the file it names to carry, so the compiled worksheet reads the same values with nothing beside it. -->
'<h4>Reading data</h4>
'Each "#read" below names a file next to this worksheet. Save this as a compiled worksheet and it keeps working: the directive carries the data, so the type, the shape and the values are the ones read here.
#read ε from strain-gauges.csv type=R_hp
#read t from sample-times.csv
'
'<h4>What was read</h4>
'Strain readings, one row per gauge -'ε
'Read as a high performance matrix -'ishp(ε)
'Gauges -'n_g = n_rows(ε)
'Samples per gauge -'n_s = n_cols(ε)
'Sample times, read as a single row -'t = t*1s
'A one row file is a matrix of one row, not a vector -'n_rows(t)
'
'<h4>Results</h4>
'The cells are written in scientific notation, which "#read" takes as it always has.
'Peak strain -'ε_max = max(ε)
'Modulus of elasticity -'E = 200GPa
'Peak stress -'σ_max = E*ε_max
'Sampling interval -'Δt = t.(1; 2) - t.(1; 1)
'Sampling frequency -'f_s = 1/Δt
